1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:13,099
This is the scribe. It writes and revises scientific text in the house style, a style built from the PI's own hand edits, and it never changes a fact.

2
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:27,199
Two jobs, inferred from what you hand it. Revise, when you give it text: it rewrites to the guide. Draft, when you give it results, figures and notes and ask for a section.

3
00:00:27,199 --> 00:00:40,100
Cutting is the main move, because most of the PI's own edits are deletions. And it touches only what needs changing, so the diff shows real edits.

4
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:51,400
It never changes a fact, a number, a citation key or a label; something that looks wrong is flagged, not corrected. It never rewraps a paragraph.

5
00:00:51,400 --> 00:01:05,099
Where a claim needs a reference it does not have, it writes a marked gap rather than inventing one. And it returns text; it never edits the manuscript itself.

6
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:14,000
A real run, on a passage written to be bad. The member asks for a revision in the house style.

7
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:30,829
The verdict is Revised. Every number survives. Each change is listed with the rule it follows, and the scribe asks a question it cannot answer: mean or median?

8
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:45,750
To use it, type to Claude Code and name it: ask the scribe to revise a passage, or to draft a section from results and notes. Say which section of the paper it is for.

9
00:01:45,750 --> 00:02:00,349
The first line is the verdict: Revised, Drafted or Gap. Then the text, and the list of what changed and why. You paste it in; the scribe does not.
